<resources>
    <!-- TV tier of the live-preview pane (see values/dimens.xml for the phone size). The
         base 240x126dp size was set for a ~360dp-wide phone column and left most of the
         ~700dp gutter beside a TV guide empty; wider here to actually use that space, and
         taller so the pane's bottom edge clears into the next channel row rather than
         cutting off partway through the row it's floating over. 16:9 to match source video. -->
    <dimen name="live_preview_width">260dp</dimen>
    <!-- 4 guide rows tall - 160dp (the 40dp min-height x4 math) rendered as 5 rows on
         device, so actual rendered row height runs closer to 32dp than the 40dp floor;
         scaled down to match. -->
    <dimen name="live_preview_height">136dp</dimen>
    <!-- Large-screen scale: everything with a smallest-width of 540dp or more - TVs, TV
         boxes, and tablets. The whole app was laid out at phone reading distance (8-13sp
         labels), which is legible in the hand and not from a sofa, so every scale steps up
         here. Held as a resource qualifier rather than an isTv branch in code so it applies
         to every view inflated from these layouts without per-view plumbing.

         Keyed on smallest-width rather than uiMode=television deliberately. dp/sp already
         normalise for resolution and density - a 1080p and a 4K panel both report ~960x540dp,
         which is why resolution itself must not change these numbers. What actually differs
         between a phone and a TV is available dp, and sw540dp captures that directly. It also
         catches the case uiMode misses: non-certified Android/IPTV boxes that report
         uiMode=normal on a television would otherwise fall back to phone sizing on a 55"
         screen. (Every common TV mode lands on exactly sw540dp: 1080p at density 2.0 and
         720p at tvdpi 1.33 both compute to 960x540dp.)

         Precedence note: smallest-width outranks uiMode, so this file wins over any
         values-television/ - keep large-screen sizing here, not there, or the two silently
         fight. isTvDevice() stays uiMode-based for behavioural branches (e.g. hiding
         downloads), which is a different question from how big to draw things.

         Padding generally *shrinks* as type grows: the phone values pad small text inside
         generously-sized boxes, so keeping them would make the boxes balloon rather than the
         text simply read bigger. -->
    <!-- +30% over the phone scale (8/10/11/12/14/17sp), no more. An earlier pass ran this
         up to 15-28sp, which is ~+65% - at that size the nav labels, pane titles and every
         form label ballooned, descriptions wrapped to three lines, and the provider rows
         squeezed their own text down to "ip / Xtr...". Settings is read while sitting still
         and interacting, not glanced at across a room like the browse grid, so it does not
         need the same headroom the poster/guide scales get. -->
    <!-- Second pass: the controls come down ~30% and the type ~18% with them. Even at the
         restrained +30% type step above, every settings pane was taller than the screen and
         had to be scrolled to reach the buttons at the bottom of it - the boxes were sized for
         a phone in the hand and then filled with sofa-distance text, so they grew in every
         direction at once. Type falls by less than the boxes do deliberately: the problem was
         never that the labels were readable, it was that each one sat in a 58dp card with
         12-20dp of air around it. -->
    <!-- Third pass: the whole settings surface at three quarters of its size. The second pass
         below took the boxes down ~30%, but on a 1080p panel the rows still read as tall empty
         cards with a label floating in them - a checkbox row was ~85dp for one line of text.
         The controls now come down another 25% (rail, gaps, card padding, targets), and the
         type with them but not as far: 8-10sp body text is not readable from a sofa, so the
         labels give up ~15% while the air around them gives up 25%. -->
    <dimen name="settings_text_micro">9sp</dimen>
    <dimen name="settings_text_caption">10sp</dimen>
    <dimen name="settings_text_body">11sp</dimen>
    <dimen name="settings_text_subtitle">12sp</dimen>
    <dimen name="settings_text_section">14sp</dimen>
    <dimen name="settings_text_title">16sp</dimen>

    <dimen name="settings_nav_icon_size">16dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="settings_nav_rail_width">142dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="settings_nav_row_min_height">26dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="settings_nav_child_min_height">23dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="settings_nav_group_indent">27dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="settings_type_card_height">28dp</dimen>

    <dimen name="settings_gap_xs">2dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="settings_gap_s">3dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="settings_gap_m">4dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="settings_gap">5dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="settings_gap_ml">6dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="settings_gap_l">7dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="settings_gap_xl">9dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="settings_column_gap">14dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="settings_pane_padding">10dp</dimen>
    <!-- A focusable D-pad target, so it stops at 28dp rather than the 0.75 of 38dp (28.5)
         being rounded down: below this the focus ring is harder to spot than the label it
         surrounds. -->
    <dimen name="settings_action_button_height">26dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="settings_qr_size">76dp</dimen>

    <!-- Holds the options column to a readable width. The pane spans the full panel now that
         the 660dp pop-out cap is gone, which left every card and input running ~700dp wide -
         a button whose label sits in the middle of half a metre of background. This is the
         only lever that narrows them: the panes are LinearLayouts, which ignore maxWidth.
         Overrides the content pane's uniform 24dp padding on the end edge only.
         40dp, not 200dp: 200dp was doing far more than holding a reading measure. On a 1080p
         panel it left a ~500dp column down the middle of the screen with a quarter of the
         width as dead space on the right, and it squeezed every provider row so hard that the
         name and URL were crushed under the row's own buttons. A margin this size still keeps
         lines off the bezel while letting the settings screen look like a TV screen. -->
    <dimen name="settings_content_inset">24dp</dimen>

    <!-- Vertical padding on settings card rows, which are wrap_content so their height is
         whatever the label needs plus this - see values/dimens.xml. -->
    <dimen name="settings_row_padding_vertical">7dp</dimen>

    <!-- Detail screen at sofa distance: the season chip is the list's primary selector, so
         it grows with the episode rows rather than staying at phone size; the 16:9 still and
         its play affordance grow with the type around them. -->
    <dimen name="season_chip_padding_horizontal">16dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="season_chip_padding_vertical">10dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="season_chip_gap">8dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="season_chip_check_size">38dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="season_chip_max_width">240dp</dimen>

    <dimen name="episode_row_min_height">112dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="episode_row_padding">12dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="episode_row_gap">10dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="episode_thumb_width">160dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="episode_thumb_height">90dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="episode_play_icon_size">40dp</dimen>

    <!-- Live TV. Channel names and numbers stay legible at couch distance while the channel
